window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][48] = "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's products are mostly funded by advertising, and it has described changes to ad measurement after platform privacy changes <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta Form 10-K for 2025 (advertising and measurement changes)]</code>, and it is described as operating large trust and safety programs (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on ads, safety and growth <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about advertiser and user tension, prevalence-based measurement of harm and growth with guardrails.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft sells enterprise software and cloud services alongside consumer products, and works with large buying organizations <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft FY2025 Form 10-K segments: Microsoft 365 Commercial and Intelligent Cloud alongside Windows, Surface, Xbox and search advertising; the point about large buying organizations is general and not in the filing]</code>. Candidates commonly report enterprise and platform questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: map buyers, users and administrators, discuss security and compliance requirements and expansion within accounts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon runs a large third-party marketplace <code>Public</code> <code>[V: European Commission decision]</code>, and is described as running a cloud platform, logistics, and programs against counterfeits and fraudulent sellers (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report marketplace trust, seller and platform questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: use the liquidity check, seller verification and abuse layers, and platform adoption by builders.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple combines devices, software and services in an integrated ecosystem and has described privacy measures that affect advertising <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy page on App Tracking Transparency (iOS 14.5, April 2021)]</code>. Candidates commonly report hardware-software, services and privacy questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about launch-once constraints, attach and on-device processing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix is a subscription entertainment service with an ad-supported tier and large content investments <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix ad tier launched 3 November 2022 (Netflix IR as reported)]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on tiers, ads and growth <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: connect tier design, ad economics (the auction view) and retention.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe provides payments and financial infrastructure for businesses, including fraud prevention and compliance tooling <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe newsroom: Connect, Billing, Terminal, Radar, Capital, Corporate Card]</code>. Candidates report payments, risk and developer-platform questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: know the payment flow, authorization versus fraud tradeoffs, compliance steps and reliability.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c27-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A consumer answer to an enterprise question</td>\n<td>Only the user is considered</td>\n<td>Map buyer, user, administrator, security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Measuring a marketplace by sign-ups</td>\n<td>&quot;Sellers signed up&quot;</td>\n<td>Liquidity: availability, conversion, utilization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Adding supply without checking demand</td>\n<td>Hosts idle, churn</td>\n<td>Check utilization after each step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ignoring compliance in fintech</td>\n<td>Onboarding with no identity step</td>\n<td>Name the required steps and their conversion cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Counting only fraud</td>\n<td>False declines absent</td>\n<td>Compare both errors with costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Measuring safety by removals</td>\n<td>&quot;We removed 2 million items&quot;</td>\n<td>Prevalence by sampling; appeals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>One threshold for all harms</td>\n<td>Same setting everywhere</td>\n<td>Set by harm and error cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ranking ads by bid alone</td>\n<td>The highest bidder always wins</td>\n<td>Weight by predicted response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Forcing adoption of an internal platform</td>\n<td>Compliance without value</td>\n<td>Adoption by value; developer experience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Copying growth tactics into regulated domains</td>\n<td>Engagement as the goal</td>\n<td>Outcomes, evidence and privacy first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c27-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints define domains</strong> (<code>MM-binding-constraint</code>). In each domain one constraint binds first: supply or demand in a marketplace, security review in enterprise sales, trust and compliance in payments, an adversary in safety. Find it before optimizing anything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Error costs differ</strong> (<code>MM-error-costs</code>). When a system can err two ways (fraud or false decline, over- or under-removal), the right threshold depends on the cost of each, not on accuracy (C23.3). Measure the cost you do not see.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodhart's law</strong> (<code>MM-goodhart</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C02). Removals, sign-ups and engagement become targets and stop measuring what matters; prevalence, liquidity and outcomes are harder to game.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To design for a domain with adversaries or risk, ask how it would fail: how a seller would cheat, how fraud would get through, how an abuser would adapt.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Payments, safety and platform work depend on reliability, latency and correctness (C21, C22); engineers will ask about failure modes, rollbacks and partner dependencies. Bring cost-of-error numbers and an incident plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> In trust-heavy domains design carries the trust (clear errors, warnings, appeals, onboarding that explains verification); in enterprise it serves administrators as well as users. Research the actors beyond the primary user.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Domain metrics need careful definition: prevalence by sampling, authorization rate by issuer, liquidity by market, NRR by cohort. Agree the definitions and the holdouts before the analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal, policy, risk and compliance.</strong> They define the requirements in payments, safety, health and education; bring them in at the problem stage and ask what the requirement is and how it can be met with the least friction.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c27-checklist-before-you-answer-inside-a-domain\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you answer inside a domain</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Have I named the actors and what each wants?</li>\n<li>Have I named the constraint that makes this domain different?</li>\n<li>Have I used the domain's vocabulary correctly, and sparingly?</li>\n<li>Have I drawn a metric tree with a first driver to investigate and a guardrail?</li>\n<li>Have I stated the tradeoff the domain forces and taken a position?</li>\n<li>Have I considered adversaries, fraud, compliance or adoption as the domain requires?</li>\n<li>Do I have a staged test and a decision rule?</li>\n<li>Have I said what I would learn first if the domain were new to me?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c27-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c27-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C27-01 · Four hundred new hosts and no new guests · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A home-sharing marketplace enters a mid-sized city. The team runs a host-recruitment campaign and signs up 400 hosts in two months, and the metric dashboard celebrates &quot;hosts signed up.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed marketing budget; a goal set on supply sign-ups; few guests in the city and little brand awareness; a three-month window before the high season.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> After three months: 400 hosts listed, 1,800 weekly searches (from existing app users and small campaigns), 45% of searches show at least one available listing, conversion given a result is 3%, so the booking rate is 1.35% and bookings about 24 a week. Utilization is 24 ÷ 400 = 0.06 bookings per host per week. Host churn is 30% within three months, and surveyed hosts say &quot;nobody books.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;Supply was chosen as the goal and the target, while demand was the binding side, so hosts are idle and leaving.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Applying the liquidity check (FW-C27-02): availability is 45% (not terrible), conversion given a result is 3% (comparable to mature cities), so the booking rate is low because there are few searches, not because results are missing. The binding side is demand (1,800 searches against 400 hosts, a low ratio); utilization of 0.06 is a fifth of a healthy 0.3. The metric (hosts signed up) rewarded the wrong thing and produced the host churn. Fixing it means demand: local awareness, guest promotions, partnerships with local events, and pausing host recruitment until utilization recovers.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep recruiting hosts to hit the supply target</td>\n<td>The sign-up metric</td>\n<td>More idle hosts; higher churn; reputational damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Pause host recruitment; spend on guest demand and events; watch utilization and availability</td>\n<td>Liquidity</td>\n<td>The supply target is missed for a quarter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Withdraw from the city</td>\n<td>Saves cost</td>\n<td>Loses the opportunity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B accepts a missed target for a healthier market; A makes the dashboard look good and hurts both sides.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Change the goal metric to bookings per week and host utilization; pause host recruitment; focus on local demand (partnerships, a guest first-booking offer); set a rule: resume recruiting when utilization exceeds 0.2 bookings per host per week and availability falls below 60%.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, after eight weeks searches rose to 4,500 a week, the booking rate to 2.4%, bookings to 108 a week, and utilization to 0.27; host churn fell to 12% in the next quarter (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If demand had not responded to the campaigns, the team would have limited the city to a narrow niche (a university area, an event venue) and tested whether depth in one area could create liquidity. The lesson: measure liquidity, find the binding side, and do not let a supply-side sign-up target decide what to do.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c27-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C27-02 · Slack's S-1, 2019: bottom-up adoption and what the numbers show · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Slack Technologies' registration statement on Form S-1 and its amendments, filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission in 2019, read on the SEC site (primary); Slack's earnings releases for the third quarter of fiscal 2020 and the first quarters of fiscal 2021 and 2022, filed as exhibits, and its annual report for fiscal 2021 (primary). Figures that circulate in commentary (free-to-paid conversion rates, pricing per user) were not confirmed in the filings read and are not used. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In its 2019 registration statement Slack said it offered a self-service approach for both free and paid subscriptions that &quot;capitalizes on strong word-of-mouth adoption&quot; and combined it with direct sales efforts aimed at growing paid users inside larger organizations; it said that self-service users become leads for its salespeople and that free usage helps prospective paid customers see the value of the product <code>[V: Form S-1]</code>. An organization counts as a Paid Customer once it has three or more users on a paid plan. As of 31 January 2019 Slack had more than 600,000 organizations with three or more users: more than 88,000 Paid Customers, including more than 65 of the Fortune 100, and more than 500,000 on the free plan <code>[V: Form S-1]</code>. It measured adoption in large enterprises by the number of Paid Customers above $100,000 of annual recurring revenue (ARR), 575 as of that date, which accounted for about 40% of fiscal 2019 revenue; the three year-ends show 135, 298 and 575 <code>[V: Form S-1 and amendment]</code>. It measured expansion within Paid Customers by its Net Dollar Retention Rate (its name for net revenue retention): 171% at 31 January 2017, 152% at 31 January 2018 and 143% at 31 January 2019, a decline it attributed to the growth of its base of revenue and to deeper penetration within existing, long-term Paid Customers <code>[V: S-1 amendment]</code>. Paid Customers numbered 37,000, 59,000 and 88,000 at those year-ends, and fiscal 2019 revenue was $400.6 million, up 82% from $220.5 million <code>[V: Form S-1]</code>. Slack's later filings and releases give 134% at the third quarter of fiscal 2020 (October 2019), 132% at 31 January 2020, 123% at 31 January 2021 and 122% at the end of the first quarter of fiscal 2022 (30 April 2021), when it reported over 169,000 Paid Customers and 1,285 above $100,000 <code>[V: earnings releases; annual report for fiscal 2021]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A product that starts with a team and spreads; a market in which large organizations buy through procurement, security review and contracts (an inference from the enterprise setting); a need to serve free users at low cost and convert some to paid; and, as the filing's risk factors note, competitors that may acquire users more rapidly or at lower cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the customer counts and their definition, the high-value customer count and its share of revenue, the net dollar retention series and the company's explanation of its decline, revenue for fiscal 2018 and 2019, and the later figures.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> An enterprise product wanted to grow by letting teams adopt it themselves, while also reaching the large organizations where most revenue sits. As an observable problem: &quot;How to grow bottom-up and still capture enterprise revenue.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>Buyers and users.</em> Usage spreads from teams (users) inside organizations, and large paid contracts require the economic buyer, security and procurement (C27.2). The company's own measures separate the two: paid customers (including many small self-serve accounts) and customers above $100,000 of ARR, where the concentration is clear: 575 of 88,000 customers (under 1%) were about 40% of revenue. That concentration means the enterprise requirements (security, administration, compliance) matter more than the customer count suggests.</p>\n<p><em>Product-led entry with a sales overlay.</em> The filing describes self-service adoption driving paid conversion and expansion, with a distinction between expansion driven by sales inside larger customers and organic, product-driven expansion inside self-serve customers. This is the hybrid motion of C20.5: the product creates the demand, and sales closes the large accounts.</p>\n<p><em>Net revenue retention as the health measure.</em> At 143%, customers that stayed grew by more than 40% a year, so the installed base alone grew rapidly, a sign of expansion within accounts (the land-and-expand arithmetic of Worked example 2). The series then falls: 171%, 152% and 143% in the filing and 134%, 132%, 123% and 122% in later documents. The company's own explanation for the first three years is a larger base of revenue and deeper penetration within existing customers, which fits the arithmetic of a base in which early gains from small accounts spreading to many users are hard to repeat at scale. Whether the later declines also reflected a saturating market, price pressure or competition is not shown by the numbers.</p>\n<p><em>What to ask next.</em> A PM reading this would ask what drives expansion (seats, products, usage), how expansion differs for self-serve and large accounts, what the retention is by cohort and size, what share of revenue depends on a few large accounts, and what the free plan costs to serve.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The company's internal conversion funnel (free to paid), the reasons for the declines after fiscal 2019 beyond the explanation it gave for 2017 to 2019, the effect of competitors' bundling, and the economics by segment. The case supports an analysis of an enterprise hybrid motion and its metrics, not a claim about why every number moved.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a company could have faced; not a record of its deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Sales-led from the start</td>\n<td>Large contracts and control</td>\n<td>Slow adoption; high acquisition cost; less word of mouth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Self-service free and paid plans with a sales overlay for large accounts</td>\n<td>Rapid adoption, low acquisition cost, then enterprise expansion</td>\n<td>Free-user cost; a slower start in large enterprises; two motions to run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Free only, monetized later</td>\n<td>Fastest adoption</td>\n<td>Revenue delay; hard to introduce charging</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B grows quickly through usage and still reaches the enterprise, at the cost of running two motions; A is slower and more controlled; C risks never charging.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the company followed B: a self-service model with free and paid plans and a sales effort in larger accounts.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: growth to more than 88,000 Paid Customers and $400.6 million of revenue in fiscal 2019, with net dollar retention declining from 171% to 143% over the three years <code>[V: Form S-1]</code> and to 122% by April 2021 <code>[V: earnings release]</code>. Later results and outcomes come from sources beyond those read here and are not claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A sales-led launch would probably have grown more slowly at a higher acquisition cost; a free-only strategy might have won users and struggled to charge. A reader should avoid hindsight: the company is one data point and its metrics are disclosed under a securities-law framework that shapes what is emphasized. The method to carry away: separate buyers from users, measure expansion with net revenue retention and concentration, expect retention to fall as the base grows, and ask what drives expansion before judging a number.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Nine questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure is invented (invented). Each ends with follow-ups and answers. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-01 · Beginner · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What makes a marketplace hard to start, and what is liquidity?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;You need both buyers and sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the cold start and defines liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures liquidity with availability, conversion and utilization, finds the binding side, and describes a start in one narrow market.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;A marketplace is worth something only if both sides are there, and each waits for the other: sellers won't list with no buyers, buyers won't come with nothing to buy. Liquidity is how likely a participant is to find a good match quickly. I measure it with the share of searches that show at least one available, relevant result, conversion once a result is shown, and how much of suppliers' capacity is used. The booking rate is the product of the first two.</p>\n<p>&quot;To start, I'd pick one narrow market (a neighborhood, a category) and make it work there before expanding. I'd find which side is short: if 60% of searches end with nothing available, supply is short; if hosts' utilization is a fifth of normal, demand is short. Then I'd add the short side in steps, checking that the other side isn't stranded.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The cold start; measured liquidity; the binding side; a narrow start.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you attract the first suppliers? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By lowering their risk: do the first transactions by hand, guarantee a minimum, bring my own demand (a partnership or a community), or offer an existing supplier a new channel with no cost. I'd measure whether the first ones transact repeatedly, which tells me whether the supply is real.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if buyers and sellers transact off the platform after meeting? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That's leakage. I'd give a reason to stay: payment protection, insurance, reviews, repeat-booking tools, and a fee that is fair for the value. If the fee is too high, they leave; I'd test it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-02 · Beginner · strategy-gtm · ST · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Who are the buyer, the user and the administrator of an enterprise product, and why does it matter?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The customer is the company and the users are employees.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Distinguishes the buyer, user and administrator and what each needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds security, procurement and champions, explains the sales cycle and non-feature requirements, and says how this changes the roadmap.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or company. &quot;The economic buyer holds the budget and cares about cost, risk and results; the users do the work in the product and care about speed and ease; the administrators configure, secure and support it and care about control, audit and reliability. Add a champion who wants it, IT and security who can block it, and procurement and legal who set terms. Together they are the buying committee, which makes the sales cycle long: months of evaluations, reviews and approvals.</p>\n<p>&quot;It matters because a product users love can still stall if it lacks single sign-on, access control, audit logs, data controls or compliance certifications, which decide deals though they are not features users ask for. So the roadmap has three streams: user value, administrator control and enterprise requirements, and the PM balances them. I'd also design for expansion: land with a team, then grow within the account, and track net revenue retention.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The actors; non-feature requirements; the cycle; roadmap consequence.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which do you prioritize when they conflict? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what blocks revenue and retention: if a security gap blocks three large deals, it takes priority over a nice user feature; if users are churning, usability does. I'd bring numbers on each and agree the tradeoff openly.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle a single large customer demanding a custom feature? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what problem it solves and whether others share it; offer a configuration or a general version; and if it must be custom, price it as a service. I'd say no to features that distort the product (C32.7).&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-03 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you reduce counterfeit and low-quality sellers in a third-party marketplace without hurting good sellers?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Verify sellers and remove bad ones.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Layers prevention, detection and enforcement, and notes false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures prevalence by sampling, sets thresholds by error cost, uses brand and buyer reports, designs appeals, and watches adaptation and fairness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and claim nothing about any real company's programs <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First measure the problem: sample 5,000 listings for expert review and find, say, 40 counterfeit, a prevalence of 0.8% with a 95% interval of about ±0.25 points; across 2 million listings that is about 16,000. Then layers. <em>Prevention:</em> verify identity and business details at onboarding, with stricter checks in high-risk categories; let brands register and gate sensitive categories. <em>Detection:</em> models on listing text, images, price and seller behavior, plus reports from buyers and brands. <em>Enforcement:</em> graduated actions (warn, limit, suspend), with appeals. <em>Incentives:</em> make counterfeits unprofitable (holds on payouts for new sellers in risky categories).</p>\n<p>&quot;Tradeoffs: stricter checks slow good new sellers and block honest ones, so I'd set thresholds by the cost of each error, use friction (extra verification) for the borderline band, and measure appeal overturn rates. Measures: prevalence (sampled), buyer complaints per 1,000 orders, time to remove, seller onboarding completion and false-suspension rate. Adversaries adapt, so I'd resample monthly and red-team the detection.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Sampled prevalence; layers; error-cost thresholds; appeals; adaptation.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A large, legitimate seller is wrongly suspended. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Fast human review, restore if wrong, and learn: why did the model flag it, and are similar sellers affected? The overturn rate is a quality signal; if it is high, tighten the model or add friction before suspension.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know the program works if counterfeits move to new tactics? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By sampling prevalence over time, not by counting removals, and by watching buyer complaints and brand reports. A rising removal count may mean better detection; a stable or falling prevalence with steady complaints means it is working.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-04 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you reduce chargebacks on a payments product without hurting conversion?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Block more risky payments.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates fraud from other disputes and compares costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments chargebacks by cause, uses friction for a borderline band, computes net benefit with the cost of lost conversion, and monitors false declines.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real product's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First understand the chargebacks: 2 million payments a month at an average $60 with a 0.45% chargeback rate is 9,000 chargebacks, and with the $15 fee about $675,000 a month. Split by cause: true fraud, 'friendly fraud' (the cardholder disputes a purchase they made), merchandise problems (not received, not as described), and unclear billing descriptors. The fixes differ: fraud needs screening; friendly fraud needs evidence and clear receipts; merchandise problems need merchant quality; descriptors need clarity.</p>\n<p>&quot;For fraud, blocking more costs good customers, so I'd add friction only for a risky band. Suppose 100,000 payments a month are in the borderline band with 4,500 of the chargebacks, and an extra verification step cuts those by 40% (1,800 × $75 = $135,000 saved) but loses 3% of the band's payments (3,000 × ($1.80 margin + $6 future value) = $23,400). Net about $111,600 a month. I'd test it on a holdout and measure conversion, chargebacks and false declines. For the other causes: clearer descriptors, receipts and delivery confirmation, and a dispute-evidence tool for merchants.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Chargebacks by cause; a friction band; net benefit with lost conversion; monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell friendly fraud from real fraud? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Signals such as repeated disputes from the same cardholder, delivery confirmation, usage logs and device history. I wouldn't treat it as certain; I'd use evidence-based dispute responses and track win rates.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if merchants resist friction? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show them the net numbers (what the step saves against what it costs), offer a setting for risk tolerance and measure their conversion impact. Merchants carry the loss, so they should have a say with the data in front of them.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-05 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How does an ad auction decide which ad to show and what it costs?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The highest bidder wins.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that predicted response is combined with the bid.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Works a numeric example with scores and the winner's price, explains why weighting protects users and revenue, and names calibration and ad-load guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Each time a slot opens, the system scores each eligible ad by its bid times the predicted chance the user will respond, which is the expected revenue per impression, and shows the highest score. The winner pays the least that would keep its position, close to what the next competitor needed. Example: A bids $2.00 with a 1.0% predicted click rate (score 0.020), B bids $3.00 with 0.5% (0.015), C bids $1.50 with 1.2% (0.018). A wins. Its price per click is C's score divided by A's rate: 0.018 ÷ 0.010 = $1.80, not $2.00. B's higher bid loses because users are unlikely to click.</p>\n<p>&quot;Weighting by predicted response raises revenue (A earns $18 per thousand impressions at its price, against $15 for B at its bid), protects users from irrelevant ads and rewards good ads. It depends on calibrated predictions; if they're wrong, revenue and quality fall. Guardrails: ad load, retention and complaints.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Expected value ranking; the price rule; a numeric example; guardrails.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not charge the winner its bid? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It encourages advertisers to shade bids and game the system; charging the minimum needed makes truthful bidding a reasonable strategy and simplifies advertisers' decisions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you add ads to a product that has none? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Start with low ad load in a few placements, measure retention against a holdout, choose formats that fit the experience, require ad quality standards, and set the guardrail that retention must not fall by more than a stated margin.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-06 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design the trust and safety approach for a new live-video feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a report button and moderators.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names harms and layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from harms and adversaries, designs prevention by design (limits, age and account requirements), real-time detection, human review, appeals and measures prevalence, and plans a staged rollout with a kill switch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Live video is hard because harm happens in real time and cannot be reviewed first. I'd start with the harms (violence, sexual content, harassment, scams, self-harm) and the adversaries who will adapt. Layers: <em>prevention by design:</em> require account age and verification to broadcast, start with small audiences, limit features for new accounts, delay or sample streams from high-risk accounts; <em>detection:</em> automated checks on audio, video and chat, with a lower threshold for severe harms; <em>response:</em> the ability to interrupt a stream quickly, human review of flagged streams within minutes, and a kill switch per feature; <em>after:</em> appeals and penalties.</p>\n<p>&quot;Measures: prevalence of violating live views by sampling, time to interrupt, proactive detection rate, appeal overturn rate and legitimate-creator friction. Tradeoffs: a lower threshold stops more harm and interrupts more good streams; I'd set it by harm severity. Rollout: invite-only, then a small share of regions, with review of incidents and red-team tests before expanding.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Harms first; layers designed for real time; prevalence; staged rollout.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you cover languages and regions? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By measuring detection quality by language, hiring reviewers who know the languages and context, and holding expansion until coverage meets the bar. Lower quality in some languages is a fairness and safety issue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if a legitimate creator is cut off by mistake? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A fast human check and restoration, an explanation, and tracking of such cases as a guardrail. If they cluster in a group, I'd treat it as a model or policy problem.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-07 · Hard · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You are the PM of an internal platform used by 40 teams. Only 12 have adopted it. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Mandate that teams use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Finds out why teams haven't adopted.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments the non-adopters, measures time to first use and the cost of migration, fixes the biggest barrier, uses early adopters as proof, and considers mandates only with evidence and a transition plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Adoption is 30% (12 of 40). I'd interview a sample of non-adopters and look at why: it doesn't fit their use case, migration is too costly, it's less reliable than their own solution, they don't know about it, or they distrust the team's support. Then quantify: time to first successful use, migration effort, and the value they'd get (time saved, incidents avoided).</p>\n<p>&quot;Fix the largest barrier first: if migration takes three weeks and the benefit is small, build migration tools or support; if it doesn't fit a use case shared by ten teams, add it; if reliability is the issue, publish and meet service levels. Use the 12 adopters as proof: collect results (hours saved) and have them help others. A mandate is a last resort: it produces compliance and resentment, and works only when the benefit is clear and a transition is funded. Measure adoption by workload moved, not by teams signed, with guardrails on reliability and customer satisfaction. I'd also be willing to say no to the teams whose needs are too different, and say so.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Diagnosis of non-adoption; barrier-first; proof; careful use of mandates.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if leadership wants a mandate by quarter-end? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show the cost and risk: forced migration of teams that won't benefit creates incidents and distrust. I'd propose a mandate for the 15 teams where the evidence is strongest, with migration help, and keep the rest on a path with a date.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you decide which requests from 40 teams to build? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By the number of teams and the value per team, the cost, and fit with the platform's purpose; publish the criteria and the roadmap, and give a clear no with an alternative to requests that don't fit.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-08 · Hard · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> As a growth PM you raised weekly active users by 12%, but 90-day retention fell. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Keep the growth; retention often drops with more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments new users and checks quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates mix shift from a real change in retention, reads cohorts by acquisition source and tactic, computes the net effect on long-term active users and acquisition payback, checks for dark patterns, and decides by a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First check whether it's mix or real. If the growth came from a new channel or tactic whose users retain less, overall retention falls even if nothing else changed; compare retention by cohort and source. Then compute the net effect on long-term active users: say a baseline of 100,000 new users with 30% retained at 90 days is 30,000 retained; the growth tactic adds 12,000 new users with 10% retention (1,200 retained) while lowering retention among existing sources by 2 points (2,000 fewer): net negative. If instead the tactic's users retain at 25% and the rest is unchanged, the net is positive.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then inspect the tactic: did it use incentives, forced sharing or hard-to-cancel flows (dark patterns, C10.10) that inflate sign-ups and harm trust? I'd check complaints and the economics: acquisition cost against the lifetime value of the retained users (C20.7). Decision rule: keep the tactic where the 90-day retained users per dollar beat the baseline channel; cut or redesign where they do not; and change the goal metric to retained users, not weekly active users, so growth is not rewarded for churn.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Mix versus real; net retained users; dark patterns; a rule; a better metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if leadership likes the weekly active user number? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show the retained-user view and the cost per retained user, and propose a dashboard with both. A metric that rises while retention falls is a warning; I'd make that visible with numbers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you test a tactic's long-term effect quickly? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Hold out a group, track early proxies of retention (day-7, day-14 activity) validated against 90-day retention in past cohorts, and run the test long enough to see them; for high-risk tactics keep a longer holdout.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c27-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C27-09 · Hard · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is different about building a product in a regulated domain such as health?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;More rules to follow.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names safety, privacy and evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs around outcomes, validates claims, engages regulators and clinicians early, handles sensitive data, plans slow cycles, and applies AI-specific evaluation and human oversight.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Three things change. The goal: outcomes (improved health measures, adherence, fewer adverse events) matter more than engagement; a product that holds attention and doesn't help is a failure. The evidence bar: claims must be supported, and a product that diagnoses or treats may be regulated as a medical device with clinical validation <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017]</code>. The data: health data is among the most sensitive, with strict rules on collection, consent, storage and sharing <code>[V: HHS: HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule apply to covered entities and business associates]</code>.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd involve clinicians, privacy and regulatory experts from the problem stage; define the claim narrowly (information, not diagnosis, unless validated); measure outcomes and safety events; design for clinical workflow, since products that burden clinicians are abandoned; and plan for slow cycles and review before launch. Growth tactics from consumer apps (aggressive notifications, streaks, incentives) can be inappropriate. If AI is involved, I'd add evaluation on real cases with error costs, human oversight for anything consequential, and clear limits shown to users (C24, C25).&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Outcomes; evidence and regulation; sensitive data; clinicians; AI controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you move quickly in such a domain? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By scoping a first release that makes a narrow, lower-risk claim, running a prospective evaluation with a small group under review, and building evidence that supports a wider claim. Speed comes from clear scope and early expert involvement, not from skipping steps.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you measure success? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Clinical or learning outcomes with a comparison group where possible, adherence, safety events, equity of access and user trust; engagement is a means and not the goal.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c27-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-01 · Liquidity</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A city has 4,000 weekly searches, 50% show an available result, conversion given a result is 4%, and 200 hosts. Compute the booking rate, bookings and utilization. If adding 50 hosts raises availability to 75% and conversion stays 4%, what are the new bookings and utilization? What do you conclude?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Marketplace.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C27-02 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Booking rate: 0.50 × 0.04 = 2%; bookings 80; utilization 80 ÷ 200 = 0.40 per host per week. After: 0.75 × 0.04 = 3%; bookings 120; utilization 120 ÷ 250 = 0.48. Utilization rose, so hosts are not stranded and the market was supply-constrained; adding the 50 hosts was justified. Check whether availability would keep rising with more hosts, and watch quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the revenue at a $100 average booking and 12% take rate: from $960 to $1,440 a week.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> If utilization had fallen below a healthy level, supply growth would have been too fast.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-02 · Net revenue retention</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A cohort pays $500,000 a year. Churned customers took $40,000; downgrades cost $10,000; expansion added $95,000. Compute NRR and say what it means.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> NRR = (start − churn − downgrades + expansion) ÷ start.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (500,000 − 40,000 − 10,000 + 95,000) ÷ 500,000 = 545,000 ÷ 500,000 = 109%. The existing customers grew 9% with no new customers; expansion more than covered churn and downgrades. Check how concentrated the expansion is (a few large accounts or many) and how NRR differs by customer size.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute gross retention (without expansion): 450,000 ÷ 500,000 = 90%.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A high NRR can hide high logo churn if a few accounts expand a lot.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-03 · An ad auction</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Advertiser X bids $4.00 with a predicted click rate of 0.5%; Y bids $2.50 with 1.0%; Z bids $1.20 with 2.0%. Compute scores, the ranking, the winner's price per click and the winner's revenue per thousand impressions at that price.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Advertising.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Score = bid × predicted rate; price = next score ÷ winner's rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C27-06 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Scores: X 0.020; Y 0.025; Z 0.024. Ranking: Y, Z, X. Y's price per click: 0.024 ÷ 0.010 = $2.40. Revenue per thousand impressions: 1,000 × 0.010 × $2.40 = $24.00. X has the highest bid and loses; ranking by bid would show X, with 1,000 × 0.005 × $4.00 = $20.00 per thousand at its bid and a less relevant ad.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a quality factor of 1.1 for Z and recompute: Z's score 0.0264 beats Y's 0.025.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Quality weights protect users and depend on calibrated predictions.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-04 · Fraud versus false declines</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A payments product handles 500,000 payments with a 0.3% fraud rate (1,500 fraudulent). A missed fraud costs $65. Tight screening catches 80% of fraud and wrongly declines 2% of good payments; loose catches 60% and declines 0.8%. A false decline costs $5 in total. Compute the total cost of each and the break-even cost per false decline.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Fintech.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C27-04 and Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Good payments: 498,500. Tight: missed 300 × $65 = $19,500; false declines 9,970 × $5 = $49,850; total $69,350. Loose: missed 600 × $65 = $39,000; false declines 3,988 × $5 = $19,940; total $58,940. Loose is cheaper by $10,410. Break-even cost per false decline: the extra fraud caught by tight screening is worth 300 × $65 = $19,500, against 5,982 extra false declines (9,970 − 3,988): $19,500 ÷ 5,982 = $3.26. Because a false decline costs $5 (more than $3.26), loose screening wins; if it cost less than $3.26, tight would. Measure the true cost of a false decline.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a middle band with extra verification and recompute.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The result turns on an estimate you can measure with a holdout.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-05 · Prevalence and thresholds</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A surface has 2,000,000 views a day with 0.2% violating. Setting A: 60% recall, 85% precision; Setting B: 80% recall, 70% precision. Compute violating views removed, wrongly removed items, remaining prevalence for each, and say which is better for a severe harm.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Trust and safety.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> False positives = true positives × (1 − precision) ÷ precision.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C27-05 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Violating views: 4,000. A: removed 2,400; false positives 2,400 × 0.15 ÷ 0.85 = about 424; remaining 1,600 (0.08%). B: removed 3,200; false positives 3,200 × 0.30 ÷ 0.70 = about 1,371; remaining 800 (0.04%). B halves the remaining harm and triples wrongful removals (424 to 1,371). For a severe harm, B (or stricter) is justified with fast appeals; for a mild category, A may be better. Set per harm and measure the appeal overturn rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute reviewers needed at 100 flagged items a day each: A about 28 (2,824 flagged), B about 46 (4,571 flagged).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Lower prevalence costs more false removals and more reviewers.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-06 · An enterprise buying map</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in an enterprise buying map (FW-C27-03) for selling a team-chat product to a 5,000-person company: the roles, each one's concern, and a plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C27-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Economic buyer (head of a function or IT):</em> cost, risk, results; evidence: a pilot with measured time saved. <em>Champion (a team lead):</em> wants adoption; arm with a pilot plan and a usage dashboard. <em>Users:</em> speed and ease; get them into a free pilot. <em>Administrators:</em> control, user management, audit; show the admin console and logs. <em>IT and security:</em> single sign-on, data location, compliance certification, retention; provide the security documentation early. <em>Procurement and legal:</em> price, terms, data-processing agreement; a standard contract and a clear price list. <em>Plan:</em> land with one team (30 days), prove results, complete the security review in parallel, then expand by department.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a competitor already in use and a plan to coexist.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Starting security review early slows the pilot and avoids a stall.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-07 · A domain metric tree</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Draw a metric tree (FW-C27-07) for a ride-hailing marketplace: the top metric, first-level drivers, the driver you would investigate first if ride completions fell, and one guardrail.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Marketplace metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C27.10 and C05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Top metric: completed rides per week. Drivers: ride requests × share fulfilled; requests = active riders × requests per rider; fulfilled share = driver availability × match rate × (1 − cancellations). Investigate first: the share of requests fulfilled, split by area and hour: a fall there points to driver supply or matching, whereas a fall in requests points to demand. Guardrails: rider wait time, driver earnings per hour (a supply guardrail), cancellations and safety incidents. If requests held and fulfillment fell, check driver supply by area and the cancellation reasons.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add price: surge and fares affect both requests and driver supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A broader tree finds more causes and slows the first check.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c27-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C27-08 · From cards to bank transfers</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A subscription product built around card payments is moving to a market where real-time bank transfers (such as UPI in India) are widely used. List five things that change in the product and the payment design.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Regional lens.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The Regional Lens in C27.3 and C20.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <code>[V: RBI circular RBI/2023-24/88 of 12 December 2023 on e-mandates for recurring payments; this exercise answer is illustrative]</code> (1) <em>Payment method:</em> offer bank-transfer payments as a first-class option and design checkout around it. (2) <em>Recurring payments:</em> learn the mandate and authentication rules for recurring transfers; the customer may need to approve the mandate, and renewals may need alerts and limits. (3) <em>Trials:</em> the convert-after-free-trial pattern depends on being able to charge automatically; redesign the trial's consent and notifications. (4) <em>Failures and retries:</em> bank-transfer failures differ from card declines; design retries, reminders and grace periods, and measure involuntary churn. (5) <em>Compliance and data:</em> identity checks, data storage and consent rules differ; involve legal and compliance early. Also price points (C20) and receipts.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with one-time and monthly prepaid plans while the recurring flow is built.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Adapting to local rails costs time and opens a market that cards do not reach.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: marketplace, enterprise and payments.</em> Use IQ-C27-01 to IQ-C27-04 and EX-C27-01, EX-C27-02, EX-C27-04 and EX-C27-06. Answer each as a 30-minute domain question aloud, starting with the actors and the constraint.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: safety, advertising, platforms, growth and regulated domains.</em> Use IQ-C27-05 to IQ-C27-09 and EX-C27-03, EX-C27-05, EX-C27-07 and EX-C27-08. Draw a metric tree in under two minutes for each of the nine domains.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to name a domain at random and give you 90 seconds to state the actors, the binding constraint and the first metric you would check.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I name the actors and what each wants?</li>\n<li>Did I name the constraint that makes the domain different?</li>\n<li>Did I use the domain's vocabulary correctly?</li>\n<li>Did I draw a metric tree with a first driver and a guardrail?</li>\n<li>Did I state the tradeoff and take a position?</li>\n<li>Did I consider adversaries, fraud, compliance or adoption as the domain needs?</li>\n<li>Did I propose a staged test and a decision rule?</li>\n<li>Did I say what I would learn first?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the domain, the binding constraint you named, the number you computed, your score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter to the domain of your own product, and to one you do not know.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Write the domain primer card (FW-C27-01) for your product's domain: actors, constraint, concepts, metric tree, tradeoffs. Check it with a colleague who knows the domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Draw your product's metric tree (FW-C27-07) with real numbers where you have them; find the driver with the weakest data. For a marketplace, compute liquidity by market; for enterprise, NRR by cohort; for payments, authorization rate by issuer; for safety, prevalence by sampling.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Pick a domain you do not know and write a one-page primer from public sources (regulator pages, company filings, engineering blogs), with three questions you would ask an expert. Use it to answer one practice question aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the primer card and tree for your domain (a time you learned a domain quickly), the measured liquidity, retention or prevalence analysis (a time you used a domain metric), and the one-page primer for a new domain (a time you ramped up). Keep numbers real and credit partners.</p>\n<span id=\"c27-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c27-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In any domain, name the actors, what each wants and the binding constraint: supply or demand in a marketplace, security review and the buying committee in enterprise software, trust, compliance and false declines in payments, an adapting adversary in safety, calibrated prediction and ad load in advertising, launch-once limits in devices, adoption in internal platforms, retention in growth, and outcomes and evidence in regulated domains.</li>\n<li>Measure what the domain actually cares about: liquidity (availability, conversion, utilization), net revenue retention and concentration, authorization rate with fraud and false declines, prevalence by sampling, revenue per thousand impressions with user guardrails, and outcomes.</li>\n<li>Where a system can err two ways, set the threshold by the cost of each error and measure the cost you do not see; add friction for the borderline band instead of one hard line.</li>\n<li>Use the domain's mechanics correctly: an ad auction ranks by bid times predicted response and prices at the next competitor's level; an enterprise sale is a buying committee and a long cycle; a payment moves through authorization, capture and settlement with fraud and chargebacks.</li>\n<li>Draw a metric tree for the named domain in two minutes, name the first driver to check and a guardrail, and say what you would learn first if the domain were new.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c27-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Slack Technologies' Form S-1 and amendments (2019), its earnings releases for fiscal 2020 to 2022 and its annual report for fiscal 2021, on a self-service enterprise motion and its metrics. Sources for CS-C27-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</li>\n<li>Andrew Chen, <em>The Cold Start Problem</em> (2021), on marketplaces and network effects <code>[V: Harper Business, 7 December 2021, ISBN 9780062969743]</code>.</li>\n<li>Bessemer Venture Partners and similar investor writing on net revenue retention and enterprise metrics, as secondary reading <code>[V: Bessemer Venture Partners, Scaling to $100 Million: net retention benchmarks for cloud companies]</code>.</li>\n<li>Stripe's public documentation on payments, disputes and fraud prevention, as primary reading on how a payments platform describes its flow <code>[V: Stripe API documentation on idempotency, versioning and webhooks]</code>.</li>\n<li>Google's, Meta's and others' published transparency reports on how prevalence of violating content is measured, read with the date in mind <code>[V: Meta, Community Standards Enforcement Report (quarterly; prevalence is the estimated share of views that were of violating content); other companies' reports were not rechecked]</code>.</li>\n<li>Hal Varian's writing on ad auctions (for example &quot;Position Auctions,&quot; <em>International Journal of Industrial Organization</em>, 2007) and Google's public documentation of its ad ranking, as an introduction to auction design <code>[V: Varian, Position Auctions, International Journal of Industrial Organization 25 (2007), pp. 1163 to 1178; Google Ads Help Center, About Ad Rank]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c28\"></span>\n<h2>C28 — Teardown Library</h2>\n<span id=\"c28-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;What is a product you admire, and what would you change?&quot; &quot;Walk me through how this feature works and why you think it was built that way.&quot; &quot;Critique this product for me.&quot; Teardown-style prompts test taste, structure and honesty: whether you can look at a real product, say what it is for, see what it does well and badly with evidence, separate what you know from what you infer, and propose a change with its cost and a way to measure it. C11 gave the method. This chapter is a library of worked teardowns, built from public sources, to practice against and to borrow patterns from.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: product-sense and critique questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often naming the interviewer's own company's product <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. The library covers products across global markets that interviewers commonly use as examples.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you reason from evidence and label it <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a purpose stated first; observations separated from inferences and opinions; strengths named with reasons; issues ranked, not listed; a recommendation with a metric and a cost; and calibrated claims about what the company intended.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state what the product is for before you judge it.</li>\n<li>You separate what you can verify from what you infer, and say which is which.</li>\n<li>You explain why a design works, not only that it does.</li>\n<li>You rank issues by impact and fixability.</li>\n<li>You propose one change with a metric and a cost.</li>\n<li>You extract a pattern you can use on the next product.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end teardown answers: a feature list in place of an analysis, claims about internal intent stated as fact, praise without reasons, a flat list of issues, a recommendation with no cost, and numbers recalled from memory with no source.</p>\n<span id=\"c28-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c28-c28-1-selection-principles-and-label-rules\"></span>\n<h4>C28.1 Selection principles and label rules</h4>\n<p><strong>How the products were chosen.</strong> (1) They are widely used across several regions, so an interviewer is likely to know them. (2) Enough public material exists to verify the central facts from primary sources (company filings, shareholder letters, newsroom posts, peer-reviewed papers). (3) Each teaches a distinct lesson (a threshold under uncertainty, a habit loop, a pricing tier, localization, privacy as a default, a setup experience, a bold redesign). (4) The set spans consumer software, hardware, marketplaces and health, with products from Google, Meta, Netflix and Apple among them, and a mix of regions.</p>\n<p><strong>Label rules (C11.7, FW-C11-06).</strong> Every company fact carries one of three labels, and every critique claim one of three layers.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Company fact</td>\n<td><strong>Verified</strong> <code>[V: source type, date]</code></td>\n<td>Stated in a primary or reliable public source that I read; the type of source is named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company fact</td>\n<td><strong>Reconstructed</strong> <code>[R]</code></td>\n<td>My inference from public information about how or why something works; the inference is stated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company fact</td>\n<td><strong>Illustrative</strong></td>\n<td>Invented to teach; marked (invented)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critique</td>\n<td><strong>Observation</strong> <code>[Obs]</code></td>\n<td>What I saw or what the source reports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critique</td>\n<td><strong>Inference</strong> <code>[Inf]</code></td>\n<td>What I think it implies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critique</td>\n<td><strong>Opinion</strong> <code>[Op]</code></td>\n<td>What I would prefer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rules.</strong> No claim about internal knowledge, intent or numbers that no public source supports. Every figure carries a source type and a date. Facts about products change; every teardown is dated <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>, and a reader should check the current product before using one in an interview. Sources were read through search results and, where noted, are secondary; the central claims of each teardown are marked Verified only where a primary or clearly reliable source was read.</p>\n<p><strong>Source ladder (FW-C28-02).</strong> Prefer, in order: company filings and shareholder letters; the company's own newsroom and documentation; peer-reviewed papers and regulator documents; reputable press quoting named people; commentary and analysts (as inference only). A claim that rests only on commentary is Reconstructed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked to critique a streaming app, a candidate says: &quot;They obviously added the ad tier to hit their subscriber target, and it has doubled revenue.&quot; The interviewer asks how the candidate knows. The candidate has stated an internal motive and a number as facts, with no source and no label. A better answer: &quot;Verified: the company launched an ad-supported plan in November 2022 and said in a shareholder letter that ads-plan membership grew almost 70% in a quarter. Inference: the plan serves price-sensitive customers and creates an advertising business. I can't verify its motive or its revenue effect from public information.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to use a teardown.</strong> In a time-limited answer you do not need the full structure; use the critique spine (C11) in the time you have. Do not recite a teardown from this library as your own analysis of a product you have not used; use the library for patterns and practice, and form your own view from your own use.</p>\n<span id=\"c28-c28-2-eight-teardowns-and-a-pointer-to-a-ninth\"></span>\n<h4>C28.2 Eight teardowns, and a pointer to a ninth</h4>\n<p>Each teardown follows the card in FW-C28-01: goal, verified facts, reconstructed inferences, strengths, ranked issues, one change with a metric and cost, and the pattern. Teardown 9 (Airbnb) is the decision case CS-C28-02 in section 10.</p>\n<span id=\"td-c28-01\"></span>\n<h5>TD-C28-01 · Duolingo: a freemium learning habit</h5>\n<p><strong>Goal.</strong> Duolingo exists to help adults learn a language through short daily lessons; for the company it serves reach, then subscriptions. I judge it by whether learners return daily and some convert (statement of assumption).</p>\n<p><strong>Verified facts.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is free to use, with revenue from a premium subscription, advertising and in-app purchases; it describes this as a freemium model <code>[V: annual report (10-K for FY2024), 2025]</code>.</li>\n<li>In its 2021 registration statement, subscriptions were about 73% of 2020 revenue, advertising about 17% and the English test and other revenue about 10% (the English test alone about 9%); it had approximately 1.6 million paying subscribers at 31 December 2020, up from about 0.9 million a year earlier, and about 37 million monthly active users at the end of 2020 (36.7 million as reported); revenue was $161.7 million in 2020 and $70.8 million in 2019; about 1.8 million paying subscribers at 31 March 2021 <code>[V: registration statement (S-1), 2021, read on the SEC site and as quoted in press reports of it]</code>.</li>\n<li>At 31 December 2024, about 32 million daily active users had a streak of seven days or more and about 10 million had a streak of 365 days or more <code>[V: annual report (10-K for FY2024)]</code>; the company reported paid subscriber penetration of 8.8% in the fourth quarter of 2024 (8.3% a year earlier) and a ratio of daily to monthly active users of 34.7% <code>[V: Q4 2024 shareholder letter]</code>.</li>\n<li>For the quarter ended 31 December 2025 the company reported about 52.7 million daily active users and 133.1 million monthly active users (averages over the quarter) and about 12.2 million paid subscribers at period end; about 43 million daily active users had a streak of seven days or more and about 15 million a streak of 365 days or more; its annual report says about 9% of monthly active users were paid subscribers (12.2 divided by 133.1 is 9.2%) <code>[V: annual report (10-K for FY2025) and Q4 2025 shareholder letter]</code>.</li>\n<li>Gamification elements (points, streaks, friendly competition) are described by the company as designed to encourage a return to learning <code>[V: annual report, 2025]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reconstructed.</strong> <code>[R]</code> The streak turns a goal (&quot;learn Spanish&quot;) that is easy to postpone into a small daily commitment with a visible cost to breaking it; the free tier supplies the audience that the paid tier and advertising monetize; and the large share of daily users on long streaks suggests that the habit mechanic, not the lessons alone, drives return.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> <code>[Obs]</code> Lessons are short and the first session starts with a lesson, not a form. <code>[Inf]</code> Daily active to monthly active users (about 35% at the end of 2024, from the reported 40.5 and 116.7 million) is high for an education app, consistent with a habit product.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues.</strong> (1) <code>[Op]</code> A streak can reward showing up over learning: a user can keep a streak with a trivial lesson. Severity high for the stated goal (learning), low for the company's engagement goal. (2) <code>[Obs]</code> Free users see advertising (the company's filings say it earns advertising revenue from free users) <code>[V: S-1]</code>; where ads and subscription prompts sit in the flow was not checked here; <code>[Inf]</code> friction of this kind can push some users to subscribe and others to leave. (3) <code>[Op]</code> Progress toward real conversation is hard to see.</p>\n<p><strong>One change.</strong> Add a periodic, low-pressure speaking or writing check that measures proficiency, and show progress against it. <em>Metric:</em> the share of users who complete the check and 90-day retention among them, with the streak-break rate as a guardrail. <em>Cost:</em> engineering and content effort; some users may feel judged.</p>\n<p><strong>Pattern.</strong> A visible daily commitment can make habit stronger than the product's core value; make sure the habit measure and the value measure move together.</p>\n<span id=\"td-c28-02\"></span>\n<h5>TD-C28-02 · Spotify Discover Weekly: a recommendation turned into a weekly ritual</h5>\n<p><strong>Goal.</strong> Discover Weekly exists to help listeners find music they will like without searching; for the company it serves engagement and differentiation. I judge it by return and by songs saved.</p>\n<p><strong>Verified facts.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It launched in July 2015 as a personalized playlist of 30 songs refreshed every Monday <code>[V: reputable press (TechCrunch, 2016) quoting company statements]</code>.</li>\n<li>By May 2016, Spotify said more than 40 million listeners had used it and that nearly 5 billion tracks had been streamed; it gave &quot;more than half&quot; for each of three separate measures (listeners who came back the following week, who streamed at least 10 tracks from it each week, and who saved at least one song), and did not say how far these groups overlap <code>[V: Spotify press release as reproduced by press, May 2016; TechCrunch, May 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Its lead engineer, Edward Newett, said it began in 2014 as a prototype hacked together by two engineers, was tested on company employees in early 2015, and was not &quot;a big company initiative&quot;; the system looks at what a listener already plays and finds connections through user activity logs, other users' playlists, web text and audio spectrograms, and the list arrives on Monday mornings <code>[V: IEEE Spectrum, 2016, quoting the engineer]</code>. A 2015 Quartz article described other people's playlists as the main ingredient, and Newett later said streaming data and playlist data are the two major components <code>[V: Quartz, 2015; press reporting an interview, 2016]</code>; the detailed method (for example, collaborative filtering, where people who like X also like Y) is <code>Reconstructed</code>.</li>\n<li>Spotify reported that more than 8,000 artists had more than half of their listeners in the prior month come from Discover Weekly <code>[V: press quoting the company, 2016]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reconstructed.</strong> <code>[R]</code> The fixed weekly cadence creates anticipation and a reason to return every Monday; a finite list (30 songs) reduces choice paralysis; saving a song is a strong signal that feeds the next list (a feedback loop, C23.8); and cold start (new users and new songs) is handled by blending content analysis with behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> <code>[Obs]</code> A finite, personal list at a predictable time; the feature solves &quot;what do I listen to.&quot; <code>[Inf]</code> Saves and returns are measurable outcomes of the feature's goal, not just plays.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues.</strong> (1) <code>[Inf]</code> Popularity bias and narrowing: the system learns from what users like, which can reinforce existing taste (C23.8). (2) <code>[Obs]</code> A weekly list disappears when it refreshes, so a missed week loses songs (the company has discussed ways to recover old lists <code>[V: press, 2016]</code>). (3) <code>[Op]</code> Users have little control to steer the list.</p>\n<p><strong>One change.</strong> Add a lightweight control (&quot;more like this, less like this, new genres&quot;) and a small exploration share of the list. <em>Metric:</em> saves per listener and the share of listeners who play an artist new to them, with return rate as a guardrail. <em>Cost:</em> some tracks the user skips more; model and interface work.</p>\n<p><strong>Pattern.</strong> A recommendation delivered on a rhythm, with a measurable &quot;keep&quot; action, becomes a habit.</p>\n<span id=\"td-c28-03\"></span>\n<h5>TD-C28-03 · Netflix: tiers, an ad plan and paid sharing</h5>\n<p><strong>Goal.</strong> Netflix exists to deliver entertainment people subscribe to and keep; the 2022 to 2023 changes aimed to widen the price ladder and convert account sharing into revenue. I judge them by net subscriber growth, revenue and retention (assumption).</p>\n<p><strong>Verified facts.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The ad-supported plan, Basic with Ads, launched in the United States on 3 November 2022 at $6.99 a month (a few other markets launched on slightly different dates); at launch the ad-free plans were Basic $9.99, Standard $15.49 and Premium $19.99 <code>[V: press reports of the company's announcement, October and November 2022]</code>. The Q3 2023 letter still lists the US ads plan at $6.99 and Standard at $15.49 <code>[V: shareholder letter, October 2023]</code>.</li>\n<li>The company's Q4 2022 shareholder letter said it expected a &quot;cancel reaction&quot; in each market when it rolled out paid sharing, based on experience in Latin America, and expected a different pattern of net additions through 2023 <code>[V: shareholder letter filed with the regulator, January 2023]</code>.</li>\n<li>In May 2023 it launched paid sharing in more than 100 countries, representing more than 80% of its revenue base; its Q2 2023 letter said revenue in each region was higher than before launch and sign-ups exceeded cancellations; paid net additions were 5.9 million in the quarter <code>[V: shareholder letter, July 2023]</code>.</li>\n<li>Its Q3 2023 letter said ads-plan membership was up almost 70% quarter over quarter and that 30% of sign-ups in ads countries were to the ads plan <code>[V: shareholder letter, October 2023]</code>.</li>\n<li>In October 2023 the company said that in the United States the ad-free Basic plan would become $11.99 and Premium $22.99, with the ads plan and Standard unchanged <code>[V: shareholder letter, October 2023]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reconstructed.</strong> <code>[R]</code> The ad plan creates a lower entry price for price-sensitive households and a new revenue stream; raising the price of the ad-free Basic plan widens the gap and nudges customers toward the ad plan; paid sharing turns users of shared accounts into either paying members or extra-member add-ons.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> <code>[Obs]</code> A staged rollout with the company stating its expectation (a cancel reaction) before it happened, and reporting results afterward. <code>[Inf]</code> A price ladder lets different customers find a fit (C20.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Ranked issues.</strong> (1) <code>[Inf]</code> Cannibalization risk: some premium customers move to the ad plan. (2) <code>[Op]</code> Account-sharing enforcement can feel like a penalty to loyal customers. (3) <code>[Inf]</code> Ad load and measurement affect the experience and advertiser trust.</p>\n<p><strong>One change.</strong> Publish clearer guidance on what each tier includes and on extra-member pricing, and measure complaints. <em>Metric:</em> tier mix, churn by tier and complaint rate per 1,000 members, with net adds as the outcome. <em>Cost:</em> simpler packaging reduces some flexibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Pattern.</strong> Tiers and price enforcement are product decisions; say the expected downside before launching and report what happened.</p>\n<span id=\"td-c28-04\"></span>\n<h5>TD-C28-04 · Google Maps two-wheeler mode: designing for how a market actually travels</h5>\n<p><strong>Goal.</strong> Google Maps exists to help people get where they are going; two-wheeler mode serves riders whose needs differ from drivers'. I judge it by the accuracy of routes and arrival times for that segment.</p>\n<p><strong>Verified facts.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Google launched an India-first &quot;two-wheeler mode&quot; on 5 December 2017 at its Google for India event <code>[V: press covering the announcement, December 2017]</code>.</li>\n<li>Google's stated rationale, from its Next Billion Users lead, was that India is the largest two-wheeler market and that riders have different navigation needs than drivers of cars <code>[V: press quoting the Google executive, December 2017]</code>.</li>\n<li>The mode shows routes that use shortcuts not accessible to cars and trucks, provides customized traffic and arrival-time estimates, and shows major landmarks along the route because many people rely on landmarks for navigation <code>[V: Google statement as reported, December 2017]</code>.</li>\n<li>It required a recent version of the Android app at launch <code>[V: press, December 2017]</code>, and Google said it would come to more countries; by June 2018 a Google executive said it had reached Malaysia, the third country after India and Indonesia, and that it took a year to develop <code>[V: press, June 2018]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reconstructed.</strong> <code>[R]</code> The product needed different routing rules (roads allowed to motorcycles), different speed and traffic models (two-wheelers move differently in traffic) and a different interface cue (landmarks); this is a segment-specific model, not a cosmetic change.</p>\n";
